# The virtualenv is ~5 GB and fully rebuildable -- the exact install command,
# including the CUDA index needed for the RTX 5060, is in PROJECT_CONTEXT_4.md
# Section 6. Never commit it.
.venv/

# A second, isolated environment holding DeepFilterNet and a CPU-only torch,
# used to benchmark an external system on our test set. It is separate on
# purpose: DeepFilterNet pins its own torch versions, and installing it into
# .venv could downgrade the CUDA 12.8 build the RTX 5060 requires. 672 MB and
# fully rebuildable -- see the note in scripts/benchmark_external.py.
.venv_dfn/

# A third isolated environment: Python 3.12 + TensorFlow, holding YAMNet for
# the semi-automatic noise pre-labelling work. Separate for two reasons --
# .venv is Python 3.14, which TensorFlow does not support at all, and TF would
# fight torch over numpy/protobuf in a venv that is carefully pinned to a CUDA
# build the RTX 5060 needs. Rebuildable: see scripts/yamnet_probe.py.
.venv_yamnet/

# Derived output of scripts/yamnet_probe.py: the per-clip report and the
# 1024-d embeddings. Regenerable in one command from the clips in dataset/,
# so it is not history -- but the report IS a finding, so if it turns up
# something worth keeping, copy the conclusion into a doc rather than
# committing the artifact.
yamnet_probe_out/

# Python bytecode
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]

# Editor and OS noise
.vscode/
.idea/
Thumbs.db
desktop.ini

# Generated figures -- reproducible in seconds from scripts/visualize_pipeline.py,
# ~1 MB each, and one gets written per mixture per build step. Keeping them
# would bloat the repo with output that carries no information the code does not.
logs/figures/

# Training runs: keep the RECORD, drop the BULK.
# config.json and metrics.jsonl are small and are the actual history of what
# was tried and what happened -- worth versioning. Checkpoints are ~12 MB
# each and samples accumulate per evaluation; both are regenerable by
# re-running, and would bloat the repo within a few experiments.
runs/*/checkpoints/
runs/*/samples/
runs/*/metrics.csv

# Safety copies of trained weights taken before a risky change. These are the
# one thing git does NOT protect -- runs/*/checkpoints/ is ignored above, so a
# checkpoint exists in exactly one place on disk. Backed up as files and hashed
# before anything risky starts. Not committed: they are ~37 MB each and are
# byte-identical to a file already on disk, so committing them would double the
# storage without adding a second point of failure.
backups/

# Generated demo audio -- rebuilt by scripts/make_demo_clips.py
generated_demo_clips/

# Pi deployment staging -- copies of tracked files and downloaded wheels.
# Both are rebuilt by the steps in deploy/RUNSHEET.md; committing 359 MB of
# duplicates would bloat the history permanently.
deploy/bundle/
deploy/wheels/
